  "hints": [
    "The answer can never be longer than the shortest word. Where do the words first disagree?",
    "Insert every word into a trie. The shared prefix is the path from the root along which nothing ever branches.",
    "Walk down from the root while the current node has exactly one child and no word ends there; the letters you pass are the answer."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function longestSharedPrefix(words) {\n  if (!words.length) return ''\n  const root = { next: new Map(), end: false }\n  for (const word of words) {\n    let node = root\n    for (const ch of word) {\n      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, { next: new Map(), end: false })\n      node = node.next.get(ch)\n    }\n    node.end = true\n  }\n  let prefix = ''\n  let node = root\n  while (!node.end && node.next.size === 1) {\n    const [ch, child] = node.next.entries().next().value\n    prefix += ch\n    node = child\n  }\n  return prefix\n}\n",
    "py": "def longest_shared_prefix(words):\n    if not words:\n        return \"\"\n    root = {}\n    for word in words:\n        node = root\n        for ch in word:\n            node = node.setdefault(ch, {})\n        node[\"$\"] = True\n    prefix, node = [], root\n    while len(node) == 1 and \"$\" not in node:\n        ch, node = next(iter(node.items()))\n        prefix.append(ch)\n    return \"\".join(prefix)\n",
    "explanation": "In a trie, all words share the path from the root until the first node that has two children or where some word ends. Walking that single-child chain spells out the longest common prefix. The '$' end marker cannot clash with a letter because words are lowercase."
  },
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(S)",
    "space": "O(S)",
    "notes": "S is the total number of characters. Comparing columns of characters directly (vertical scanning) is also O(S) time with O(1) extra space; the trie pays off when you must answer many prefix questions about the same set."
  },
  "followUps": [
    "Words arrive one at a time and you must report the shared prefix after each new word. What would you keep between updates?"
  ]
}
